2026-09-14T18:07:32.9100786Z             "body": "Correcting the exhaustive test timestamps exposed populated-query failures that empty Loki responses had hidden. This PR fixes literal filters, query-created regexp captures, quantile grouping and windows, ordered JSON metric errors, additive sums, and binary matching against real Loki 3.7.7 fixtures.\n\nThe branch is rebased onto v1.67.0 metadata merge `ee0d526`; the security integration from #525 is already in main and is not a second merge of that work.\n\n### Behavior\n\n- Preserve exact labels, values and evaluation timestamps, including cache repeats and window boundaries.\n- Execute binary operands through scoped handlers, preserving tenant context and upstream errors; retain grouping, comparison semantics and explicit extraction aliases.\n- Retain native aggregation when it is provably equivalent, and bound raw execution when exact samples are required.\n- Reject invalid IP arguments, invalid vector matching and excessive work explicitly. Corrected results and new resource errors are observable compatibility changes.\n\n### Validation and remaining work\n\nStrict unique-fixture API regressions run against Loki and VictoriaLogs and are selected by `scripts/ci/run_security_regressions.sh`. Local package/race checks and the focused live suites have passed during implementation. Final verification must run on the final PR head.\n\n**Draft:** final review found additional escaped-string/empty-grouping round-trip issues and an unbounded bare-parser metric path. Those follow-up fixes and regressions are being finalized locally, followed by the complete race/lint suite, a fresh Compose compatibility run and Grafana Explore/Drilldown acceptance. The current draft is not ready to merge.\n\nThe v1.67.0 release was rebuilt from its actual merge commit. Its long-running local UI pass exposed resource pressure, failed paired ingestion and OOM-killed services; those runs are recorded as failures and do not establish full compatibility. The final follow-up pass uses fresh paired fixtures. Known unsupported syntax and intentional resource limits remain documented rather than being counted as successful parity.\n",
